<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Montferrat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Monferrato</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Montferrat (Monferrato) is a hilly region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, between the Po and the Ligurian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a>. It was ruled by the Aleramici and then the Palaiologos marquises, passed to the Gonzaga of Mantua in 1536 and went to Savoy in the early 18th century. Known for wines such as Barbera, its vineyard landscapes are a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montferrat.jpg" alt="Montferrat" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frukko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Michele_di_Costigliole_d%27Asti.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Montferrat, in Italian Monferrato and in Piedmontese Monfrà, is a historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> whose extent has varied over time. It covers roughly the modern provinces of Alessandria and Asti. The river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanaro">Tanaro</a> cuts it in two: the northern part, the Basso Monferrato, between the Tanaro and the Po, is an area of rolling hills and plains, while the southern Alto Monferrato rises from the Tanaro into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and the water divide between Piedmont and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a>. The Monferrato Astigiano, which includes almost all of the Province of Asti, is hilly, with towns such as Nizza Monferrato and Canelli, and its highest point is the hill of Albugnano at 549 metres.</p><p>The origin of the name is uncertain. Proposals derive it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> mons ferax, fertile mountain, from mons ferratus, for iron left by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, or from farro, a variety of wheat. A legend says that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleramo">Aleramo</a>, the founder of the march, shod a horse with a brick, mon in the local dialect, so that the horse was fra, shod.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Originally a county, Montferrat became a margravate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> under Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleramo">Aleramo</a> in 961, as power in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berengar-ii-of-italy">Berengar of Ivrea</a> to Otto I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Its marquesses and their families were related to the Kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperors</a>, took part frequently in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>, and intermarried with the royal family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and the Byzantine imperial houses of Comnenus, Angelus and Palaiologos.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> controlled the march briefly between 1533 and 1536, after which it passed to the Gonzaga dukes of Mantua, who held it until 1708. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Maximilian II, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, raised it to a duchy in 1574. A piece of the duchy went to the Duchy of Savoy after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-mantuan-succession">War of the Mantuan Succession</a> of 1628 to 1631, and the remainder passed to Savoy in 1708, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-holy-roman-emperor">Leopold I, Holy Roman Emperor</a>, took possession of the Gonzaga territory. The next Gonzaga heir was later compensated with the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Teschen</a> in Silesia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Montferrat is one of the most important wine districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, known for red and sparkling wines such as Barbera d'Asti, Asti Spumante and Moscato d'Asti, and with Alba for truffles. Its dry continental climate and soils favour viticulture. The Sacro Monte di Crea, a Roman Catholic sanctuary begun in 1589 around an older Sanctuary of St. Mary, is one of the nine Sacri Monti of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> on the World Heritage list, and in 2014 Montferrat itself was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Writers from the region include Vittorio Alfieri of Asti and Umberto Eco of Alessandria.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Montferrat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Federico II Gonzaga, its first duke, Mantua later became a duchy, which in 1531 also acquired <b>Montferrat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-16">16 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>John VIII Palaiologos, eldest son of Emperor Manuel II, married Sophia of <b>Montferrat</b> and was crowned co-emperor, assuming full power.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-19">19 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Governed by consuls and later a podestà, it fought its neighbours, especially the marquises of <b>Montferrat</b>, for control of the surrounding plain.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-alessandria">Commune of Alessandria</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gonzaga ruled as imperial vicars, became marquises in 1433 under Gianfrancesco Gonzaga and dukes in 1530 under Federico II, who also acquired <b>Montferrat</b>, later the Duchy of Montferrat.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mantua">Duchy of Mantua</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Montferrat</b> was a hilly wine-growing region split into two parts and surrounded by the Duchy of Savoy and the Duchy of Milan.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-montferrat">Duchy of Montferrat</a></li><li><blockquote>In 967 Otto I confirmed Aleramo, a nobleman of Frankish descent, as margrave of lands in the hills south of the Po between Turin and Genoa, later called <b>Montferrat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-montferrat">March of Montferrat</a></li></ul></section>
